1\.4\.1\.a 

__Overview__

- General \- Color must not be used as the sole method of conveying content or distinguishing visual elements\.
- HTML \- Color must not be used as the sole method of conveying content or distinguishing visual elements\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Carefully observe the page \(requires vision and not being color blind\)\. 
2. Look for any item on the page where color is used to convey information\.
3. Check that the information conveyed by color is also conveyed visually without relying on color, e\.g\. via a symbol or text or font weight or pattern, etc \(for the color blind\) and an accessible text or programmatic alternative \(for screen reader users\)\. 
	- __Note:__ If the contrast ratio between elements that are distinguished by color is 3:1 or greater, then the information is not based on color\-alone because the contrast is great enough to visually distinguish the difference, AS LONG AS the colors have no meaning in themselves \(e\.g\. green = good, red = bad\)\.

__Things to think about__

- Use this checkpoint to log issues when a button's focus state is only indicated by a change in the background color of the button, and the difference of the contrast of the default and focused states is less than 3:1\. If a button's focus state is indicated by text or icon contrast changes which are at least 3:1, then it passes 1\.4\.1\.
- Use this checkpoint to log issues when a link's focus state is only indicated by a change in the text color of the link, and the difference of the contrast of the default and focused states is less than 3:1\.
- __Reality Check 1:__ I could print the page in black and white and still have access to all the information and/or functionality conveyed by color\.
- __Reality Check 2:__ I could use the page with a screen reader \(and no vision\) and still have access to all the information and/or functionality conveyed by color\.

For example, the list item displayed in purple below indicates the most popular keynote speaker at SXSW\. But, purple text is not sufficient to make the content accessible\. This information needs to also be conveyed in text\. 

SXSW Keynote Speakers\. Most popular speaker is listed in purple\. 

- Bruce Sterling 
- Jane McGonigal 
- Merlin Mann 

To make the example accessible, add explanatory text: 

SXSW Keynote Speakers\. Most popular speaker is listed in purple and identified as such\. 

- Bruce Sterling 
- Jane McGonigal \(most popular\) 
- Merlin Mann 

__Issue Descriptions__

- Color is used as the sole method to identify error\(s\) on form field\(s\)\. \(form\-errors\-color\-only, "issue", serious\)
- Color is used to convey information that is not conveyed in any other way\. \(information\-color, "issue", serious\)
- A change in color is used to visually convey the state of a control, and the contrast difference between the states is less than 3:1\. \(state\-color\-only, "issue", serious\)

1\.4\.1\.b

__Overview__

- General \- Color alone must not be used to distinguish links from surrounding text unless the contrast ratio between the link and the surrounding text is at least 3:1\.
- HTML \- Color alone must not be used to distinguish links from surrounding text unless the contrast ratio between the link and the surrounding text is at least 3:1\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Look for text links that are located within a paragraph or other block of text, for example text links that are in a sentence or a list\. 
	- __Note:__ Links in obvious navigation areas \(header, main nav, side nav, footer\) do NOT need to meet this requirement\.
	- __Note:__ Even if a link has been given another programmatic role, such as role="button", this checkpoint still applies\.
2. Determine if the link text is differentiated from surrounding non\-link text by an indicator other than color\. 
	- __Note:__ Links that are the __same__ color as surrounding text are not using color to distinguish the link text from static text and therefore do __not__ fail this checkpoint, with or without other differentiation\.
	- __Note:__ There are many ways to visually differentiate a link from surrounding non\-link text beyond underlining the link\. Other common options include: bolding, italics, change in font family, a chevron, arrow, outline, or other visual indicator\.
	- If there is an additional visual differentiator, this checkpoint is passed\.
3. If there is no other visual differentiation, check that the color of the link text differs from the color of the surrounding non\-link text by a color contrast ratio of at least 3:1\. 
	- Get the color of the link text\. 
		1. In Chrome, right click on the link and select "Inspect"
		2. Get the hex value for the color of the link\.
	- Get the color of the non\-link text\. 
		1. In Chrome, right click on any text that is not a link \(and representative of the color of the non\-link text surrounding this link\) and select "Inspect"
		2. Get the hex value for the color of the non\-link text\.
	- Determine the contrast between these two colors\. 
		1. Enter the link color and the non\-link color in a color contrast tool like the [Deque Color Contrast Analyzer [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://dequeuniversity.com/color-contrast)](https://dequeuniversity.com/color-contrast)to get the contrast ratio\.
		2. If the two colors have a contrast ratio of 3:1 or greater, this checkpoint is satisfied\.
		3. __Note:__ If the color of the link text and the color of the non\-link text are exactly the same, color is NOT being used to differentiate the link text and therefore, this checkpoint is satisfied\.

__Reality check:__ If I were color blind and could not tell the difference between link text and plain text, where people with good color vision could, I could still identify the links within the non\-navigational area of the page\.

__ssue Descriptions__

- Link text and static text are differentiated only by color\. The contrast ratio between default link text and surrounding text is not at least 3:1\. \(link\-insufficient\-contrast, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

- Provide additional link indicator on hover \- Links should be differentiated from the surrounding text by both a color ratio of at least 3 to 1 and an additional indicator \(like an underline\) on hover\. \(link\-no\-mark, "best\-practice", moderate\)

__2\.4\.1\.a Skip Navigation, Headings or Landmarks__

__Overview__

- General \- A method must be provided to skip past blocks of content that are repeated on multiple screens\.
- HTML \- A method must be provided to skip past blocks of content that are repeated on multiple screens\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

__Important Note: Only one \(1\) of these options needs to be present to pass this success criteria\.__ Start testing for the first feature and stop when you find one\. If none of the features are present, this checkpoint fails\.

__Option 1: Skip to Main Content Link:__

1. Open the page and using your keyboard tab key, tab in the page\. Verify that one of the first focusable items on the page is a skip link, e\.g\. "Skip to Main Content"? It is okay if the skip link is not the actual first item\. Ideally it should be within the 1st \- 3rd focusable items\.
2. Test the link by activating it\. Does the focus move to the main content section of the page? Note, if there are no vertical scrollbars on the page, you will need to shrink the height of the browser to test if the link is working\. Shrink the browser window until the vertical scrollbar appears\. 
3. __Optional:__ Are there additional skip links provided e\.g\. "Skip to search", "Skip to navigation", and so on? If so, do these links take you to the area of the page as intended \(to the search feature, the main menu, etc\.\)? Do not call a failure of this checkpoint if these additional skip links are missing or broken\. Do report problems of this nature as Best Practice to fix\.

__Option 2: Semantic Heading Structure:__

Look for a semantic heading at the top of the main content area\. Often this is the h1 for the content\.

__Option 3: Landmarks__

Look for a <main> sectioning element or a role="main" ARIA landmark that wraps the main content of this page\.

__Recommended tools to reveal headings and landmarks__

Bookmarklets:

- [ANDI bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)
- [Paul Adam Headings and Landmarks bookmarklets [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)

[Web Developer browser extension [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)\(Note limited capabilities: only reveals HTML headings\):

- Outline > Show Element Tag Names
- Outline > Outline Headings

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

__Important Note: Only one \(1\) of these options needs to be present to pass this success criteria\.__ Start testing for the first feature and stop when you find one\. If none of the features are present, this checkpoint fails\.

__Option 1: Skip to Main Content Link:__

1. Open web page in Chrome with NVDA turned on\.
2. Select the NVDA key \(insert\) and F7\.
3. The Elements List should appear\.
4. Choose type Links and review the content within\.
5. Verify that one of the first few focusable items on the page is a skip link, e\.g\. "Skip to Main Content"? It is okay if the skip link is not the actual first item\. Ideally it should be within the 1st \- 3rd focusable items\.
6. Verify that the link works as you would expect\.

![Screenshot of NVDA Elements List](data:image/jpeg;base64,)

__Option 2: Semantic Heading Structure__

1. Open web page in Chrome with NVDA turned on\.
2. Select the NVDA key \(insert\) and F7\.
3. The Elements List should appear\.
4. Choose the type Headings and review the content within\.
5. Verify that there is a heading near the top of the main content that allows you to jump to the main content by selecting that heading\.

![Screenshot of NVDA Elements List with Headings chosen](data:image/jpeg;base64,)

__Option 3: Landmarks__

1. Open web page in Chrome with NVDA turned on\.
2. Select the NVDA key \(insert\) and F7\.
3. The Elements List should appear\.
4. Choose type Landmarks and review the content within\.
5. Verify that the page has a "main" landmark that allows you to jump to the main content\.

![Screenshot of NVDA Elements list showing Landmarks](data:image/jpeg;base64,)

__Issue Descriptions__

- The page does not have at least one of: a skip link, proper heading structure, HTML5 sectioning elements, or ARIA landmarks\. \(structure\-major\-problems, "issue", serious\)
- The set of software programs do not have a mechanism to move past repeated content\. \(structure\-no\-bypass\-software, "issue", serious\)
- The skip link provided does not function as expected / is broken\. \(structure\-skiplink\-broken, "issue", serious\)
- The skip link does not become visible when it receives focus\. Sighted keyboard\-only users are unable to use the link\. \(structure\-skiplink\-invisible, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

- ARIA landmarks \- ARIA landmarks should be used\. \(aria\-landmark\-not\-used, "best\-practice", moderate, March 19, 2013\)
- ARIA landmarks for every section \- All sections of the page should be defined within ARIA landmarks\. \(aria\-landmark\-outside, "best\-practice", moderate, March 19, 2015\)
- Provide labels when multiple navigation regions exist \- When multiple navigation regions are present on a page, provide aria\-labels to distinguish the purpose of each\. \(nav\-sections\-not\-identified, "best\-practice", moderate, February 28, 2019\)
- Skip link only when needed \- A skip link should not be included when there is no repetitive block of content / links on the page\. The presence of the skip link \(when it is not needed\) is confusing\. \(structure\-confusing\-skiplink, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2015\)
- Skip nav link \- A skip nav link should be available to screen reader users and keyboard only users\. While WCAG Success Criteria 2\.4\.1 can be passed with ARIA Landmarks and/or good heading structure, these methods are not as accessible to keyboard only users\. \(structure\-no\-nav\-bypass\-skiplink, "best\-practice", moderate, April 2, 2013\)
- Skip nav link first \- The skip link to the main content should be the first link on the page\. \(structure\-skiplink\-not\-first, "best\-practice", minor, April 16, 2013\)
- Skip nav link webkit \- The skip nav link should work in webkit browsers\. There is a bug in some versions of webkit\. This is a browser bug, not a WCAG failure\. \(structure\-skiplink\-webkit\-incompatible, "best\-practice", minor, April 16, 2013\)

2\.4\.1\.b Frames Titles\(Deprecated\)

__1\.4\.13\.a Content on Hover or Focus__

__Overview__

- General \- When additional content is triggered on pointer hover or on keyboard focus, that additional content is persistent, can be hovered with a mouse pointer, and is dismissible without moving hover or focus\.
- HTML \- When additional content is triggered on pointer hover or on keyboard focus, that additional content is persistent, can be hovered with a mouse pointer, and is dismissible without moving hover or focus\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Identify all the instances where additional content appears on focus or on hover\. Custom tooltips, sub\-menus, and other non\-modal popups that display on hover and focus are examples of additional content covered by this checkpoint\. 
	- Tab through the default state of the page and identify any additional content that appears on keyboard focus\.
	- Similarly, use a mouse to discover where additional content appears on mouse hover\.
	- __Out of scope:__ When the appearance of the additional content is completely controlled by the user agent such as when browsers display the title or data\-title attribute content in HTML as a small tooltip\.
2. For each instance of additional content that appears on focus or on hover, confirm that all 3 of the following requirements are true: 
	- __Dismissible:__ A mechanism \(such as clicking the trigger or pressing the ESC or Enter key\) is available to dismiss the additional content without moving pointer hover or keyboard focus, __UNLESS__ the additional content communicates an input error or does not obscure or replace other content\.
	- __Hoverable:__ If pointer hover can trigger the additional content, then the pointer can be moved over the additional content without the additional content disappearing\.
	- __Persistent:__ The additional content remains visible until the hover or focus trigger is removed, the user dismisses it, or its information is no longer valid\.

__Things to think about__

- This requirement covers content that appears in addition to the triggering component itself\. Therefore, a non\-visible component, like a Skip to Main link, that is made visible on keyboard focus \(with no additional content beyond the trigger becoming visible\) is not in scope for this checkpoint\.
- If there is additional content that can only be triggered on\-hover \(i\.e\. it cannot be triggered with a keyboard\), it should be marked as a failure of 2\.1\.1 Keyboard\.
- If something that appears on hover or focus takes focus away from the trigger element, that is a failure of checkpoint 3\.2\.1\.a\. Context Changes \(On Focus\)\. An example is a modal dialog that is opened on hover or focus \(as opposed to clicking or pressing Enter on a button\)\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

Screen reader testing is not applicable for this checkpoint\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- Additional content does not remain visible until dismissed or invalid\. \(content\-does\-not\-remain\-visible, "issue", serious\)
- Additional content that becomes available on hover does not allow the user to move the pointer over that same additional content without the additional content disappearing\. \(content\-hover\-disappears, "issue", serious\)
- Failure to make additional content dismissible without moving pointer hover or keyboard focus\. \(content\-not\-dismissible, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

__1\.4\.5\.a Images of Text__

__Overview__

- General \- If the same visual presentation can be made using text alone, an image must not be used to present that text\.
- HTML \- If the same visual presentation can be made using text alone, an image must not be used to present that text\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

Examine the page for any elements that look like an image of text\. To investigate an image that looks like it might be an image of text:

__Option 1: Use your mouse and / or code inspection: __Try to highlight the text by clicking and dragging over it with your mouse\. HTML text can be highlighted while text embedded in an image cannot, but this test is not perfect\. If you cannot highlight the text with your cursor, inspect the code to double check whether there is actual HTML text present\.

__Option 2: Use the __[__Web Developer extension [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)__](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)__:__

1. Select Images > Outline All Images
2. Check that none of the images contain informative or meaningful text\.

__Option 3: Use Firefox: __

1. Select View > Zoom > Zoom Text Only
2. Select View > Zoom > Zoom in
3. Take note of any instances in which text does not enlarge\. These instances are probably images\.

__Things to think about__

- If the information provided by the embedded text is also available in real text on the page, this checkpoint is satisfied\.
- If the information provided by the embedded text is not available anywhere else on the page AND the text visual effect is producible using HTML and CSS and a reasonably similar font, this checkpoint is failed\. 
	- __Note:__ Practically producible in HTML/CSS = Reasonably possible to position the text properly and maintain color contrast in different responsive design breakpoints\.
- Text in <canvas> elements is an image of text\.
- Whether text in <svg> elements is considered an image of text depends on how it is implemented: 
	- Text in an <svg> element using the <text> element is __not__ an image of text\. This text will minimally pass the normative requirements\. It is technically possible to change the font, size, color and background of this text using css\.
	- Text drawn with <path> in an <svg> element is an image of text\.
	- Text in a \.svg image source file is an image of text\.

__Exceptions:__

- __Essential Images of Text:__ A particular presentation of text is essential to the information being conveyed in the image\. Examples: 
	- Logotypes, branding, type/font samples
	- Graphs, screenshots and diagrams which visually convey important information through more than just text
	- A photo or graphical representation of an actual object\.
- __Incidental Text in Images:__ Text that appears in an image but is incidental to the information being conveyed in the image\. Examples: 
	- A photo of a person speaking at a conference with their name visible on their nametag
	- A photo of a car at an intersection with a stop sign visible in the background

While these images mentioned above are exempt from the Success Criteria 1\.4\.5, they will still require appropriate text alternatives to comply with Success Criteria 1\.1\.1\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

Screen reader testing is not applicable for this checkpoint\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- The image contains embedded text\. \(image\-of\-text, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

__3\.3\.1\.b Errors Identification__

__Overview__

- General \- When form input errors are identified, error messages must be provided in text and must indicate which field has an error\.
- HTML \- When form input errors are identified, error messages must be provided in text and must indicate which field has an error\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

Testing for this checkpoint can be accomplished using either the "Manual" methodology or the "Assistive Technology" methodology or a combination of both\.

1. Locate forms in the content\.
2. Fill out the form, deliberately making various mistakes such as entering user input that falls outside the required format or values or leaving required fields blank, and submit the form\. It may take several attempts to find the different failures conditions that can be triggered\.
3. If an input error is detected, verify that BOTH of the following are true: 
	1. The error message is provided in text\. Options include: 
		- Visible text on screen
		- Alternative text on an image
	2. The field that has an error is identified\. Options include: 
		- The field name is included within the text of the error message itself\. For example: "First name is required\." or "Policy start date is invalid\."
		- The error message is programmatically associated with the field \(typically via aria\-describedby, but can also be via the form <label> or aria\-label\.\)

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

1. Locate forms on a screen\.
2. Fill out the form, deliberately making various mistakes such as entering user input that falls outside the required format or values or leaving required fields blank, and submit the form\. It may take several attempts to find the different failures conditions that can be triggered\.
3. If an input error is detected, use a screen reader to verify that BOTH of the following are true: 
	1. The error message is provided in text\. Options include: 
		- Visible text on screen
		- Alternative text on an image
	2. The field that has an error is identified\. Options include: 
		- The field name is included within the text of the error message itself\. For example: "First name is required\." or "Policy start date is invalid\."
		- The error message is programmatically associated with the field \(typically via aria\-describedby, but can also be via the form <label> or aria\-label\.\)

__Issue Descriptions__

- The form field with an error is not identified in text, or text alternative, or via programmatic association\. \(form\-error\-field\-not\-identified, "issue", critical\)
- Error message cannot be read by screen readers\. \(form\-error\-unreadable, "issue", critical\)
- Input validation failures are not described in text or a text alternative\. \(form\-error\-validation\-not\-described, "issue", critical\)

__Best Practices__

- Markup each field that has a data entry error with aria\-invalid="true" \- Fields with data entry errors should be marked with aria\-invalid="true" \(aria\-invalid\-missing, "best\-practice", moderate, January 9, 2017\)
- Programmatically associate errors \- Error messages should be programmatically associated with the corresponding form field\. \(form\-error\-not\-associated, "best\-practice", serious, February 3, 2016\)
- Error message automatically announced \- Error message is available in text and screen readers automatically announce this new error text\. \(form\-error\-not\-auto\-announced, "best\-practice", serious, April 16, 2018\)
- Helpful error messages \- The error message should describe precisely why the element failed validation\. \(form\-error\-not\-descriptive, "best\-practice", serious, February 3, 2016\)
- Position error message near corresponding field \- Position error message near the corresponding field so it is easy to visually associate it with the related field, especially when zooming or magnifying the screen\. \(form\-error\-positioned\-improperly, "best\-practice", serious, November 4, 2016\)

__3\.3\.3\.a Error Suggestion__

__Overview__

- General \- When form input errors are identified, suggestions must be provided for fixing the input, unless it would jeopardize the security or purpose of the content\.
- HTML \- When form input errors are identified, suggestions must be provided for fixing the input, unless it would jeopardize the security or purpose of the content\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

Testing for this checkpoint can be accomplished using either the "Manual" methodology or the "Assistive Technology" methodology or a combination of both\.

1. Locate any forms in the content\.
2. Complete every form control, deliberately entering user input that falls outside the required format or values and submit the form\. It may take several attempts to find the different failures conditions that can be triggered\.
3. If an input error is automatically detected, verify that the error message gives a suggestion about how to fix it\. Appropriate error suggestions include: 
	1. Indicating a required field has been left blank\.
	2. Indicating the required format for the field in error\.
	3. Indicating a range of valid values for the field in error\.
4. __Exception:__ If providing an error suggestion would jeopardize the security or purpose of the content, then error suggestions are not required\. For example, login and password fields are not required to provide error suggestions because doing so would weaken security of the login process\.

__Reality Check:__ If I submit a form using incomplete and/or incorrect data formatting, the error message\(s\) returned tell\(s\) which fields were in error, why, and I have a clue of how to fix it\.

__Things to think about__

- SC 3\.3\.1 Error Identification \(Level A\) checks for the following: the invalid entry has a text description that indicates it is incorrect and identifies the field with the error\. This checkpoint ensures the error message also provides information on how to fix the error\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

1. Locate any forms in the content\.
2. Complete every form control, deliberately entering user input that falls outside the required format or values and submit the form\. It may take several attempts to find the different failures conditions that can be triggered\.
3. Using a screen reader, verify that the error message gives a suggestion about how to fix it, if it is possible to do so, for example: 
	1. Indicating a required field has been left blank\. OR
	2. Indicating the required format for the field in error\. OR
	3. Indicating a range of valid values for the field in error\.
4. __Exception:__ If providing an error suggestion would jeopardize the security or purpose of the content, then error suggestions are not required\. For example, login and password fields are not required to provide error suggestions because doing so would weaken security of the login process\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- The error message does not indicate expected data format\. \(form\-error\-format, "issue", serious\)
- The error message does not indicate expected range of data values\. \(form\-error\-range, "issue", serious\)
- There is no suggestion for how to fix a validation failure\. \(form\-error\-unhelpful, "issue", serious\)

__3\.3\.4\.a Error Prevention \(Legal, Financial, Data\)__

__Overview__

- General \- If a user action can cause an important legal, financial, test/exam, or unrecoverable/unchangeable user data transaction to occur, the changes/deletions must be reversible, verified, or confirmed\.
- HTML \- If a user action can cause an important legal, financial, test/exam, or unrecoverable/unchangeable user data transaction to occur, the changes/deletions must be reversible, verified, or confirmed\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Determine if there are any actions on the page where a user action can cause a legal commitment, financial transaction, test/exam submission, or modification or deletion of user controlled data to occur\. 
2. If there is, confirm that AT LEAST ONE of following is true\. The transaction is: 
	- __Reversible: __The submission is reversible\.
	- __Checked:__ Data entered by the user is checked for input errors and the user is provided an opportunity to correct them\.
	- __Confirmed:__ A mechanism is available for reviewing, confirming, and correcting information before finalizing the submission\.

__Things to think about__

- This check applies to significant transactions or submissions or deletions\. It is not intended to require a confirmation for each save command or the simple creation or editing of documents, records, or other data\.
- Examples of transactions covered include: 
	- Purchasing airline tickets\.
	- Submitting an order to purchase stock in a brokerage account\.
	- Deleting a travel profile in a travel services web site\.
	- Deleting a record of past invoices\.
	- Submitting answers to an online certification exam\.
	- Signing a contract to rent a house\.
- Examples of solutions to meet this requirement: 
	- Providing a stated time within which an online request \(or transaction\) may be amended or canceled by the user after making the request\.
	- Providing the ability for the user to review and correct answers before submitting\.
	- Providing a checkbox for a user to indicate they have reviewed their input and are ready for it to be committed\.
	- Providing the ability to recover deleted information\.
	- Requesting confirmation to continue with selected action\.

__Reality Check:__ If I am getting ready to submit a form or initiate an action that has real consequences to me \(such as financial or legal commitments\), I am given a chance to review and change or cancel the transaction\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

Follow the testing methodology for Desktop Web \- Manual\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- The "Delete"/"Cancel" button immediately triggers the deletion of the legal, financial, or data transaction on the database\. Users do not have the ability to recover from an accidental deletion\. \(form\-accidental\-submission\-recovery, "issue", serious\)
- Users are not given the opportunity to review and edit information before the data is submitted\. \(form\-review, "issue", serious\)
- User does not get an opportunity to review and confirm the transaction before committing it\. Nor is the action reversible\. \(form\-review\-recovery, "issue", serious\)

__3\.2\.1\.a Context Changes \(on Focus\)__

__Overview__

- General \- When an element receives focus, it must not result in a substantial change to the screen, the spawning of a pop\-up window, an additional change of keyboard focus, or any other change that could confuse or disorient the user\.
- HTML \- When a page element receives focus, it must not result in a substantial change to the page, the spawning of a pop\-up window, an additional change of keyboard focus, or any other change that could confuse or disorient the user\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

Testing for this checkpoint can be accomplished using either the "Manual" methodology or the "Assistive Technology" methodology or a combination of both\.

1. Navigate or tab through all the user interface components on the page using a keyboard\.
2. Make sure you do not encounter any unexpected context changes as any of the page components receive focus\. \(See "Things to think about" below for a definition of "context change"\.\)

__Things to think about__

- __Definition of "context change"\.__ A change of context is a change of: 
	- User agent
	- Viewport
	- Focus
	- Content that changes the meaning of the current screen
- __A change of CONTENT is NOT always a change of CONTEXT\.__ Changes in content, such as an expanding outline, opening a datepicker, dynamic menu, or a tab control do not necessarily change the context, unless they also change one of the above \(e\.g\., focus\)\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

1. __Using a screen reader__, navigate or tab through all the user interface components on the page using a keyboard\.
2. Make sure you do not encounter any unexpected change of context as any of the page components receive screen reader focus\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- Loading the page causes another window to open up unexpectedly and gain focus\. \(focus\-moves\-on\-load, "issue", serious\)
- Form is automatically submitted when a component receives focus\. \(focus\-submits\-form, "issue", critical\)
- When a component receives keyboard focus, focus is automatically removed or redirected\. \(focus\-visual\-affects\-keyboard, "issue", critical\)
- When screen reader focus falls on a component, focus is automatically removed or redirected\. \(focus\-visual\-affects\-screen\-reader, "issue", critical\)
- When switch control focus falls on a component, focus is automatically removed or redirected\. \(focus\-visual\-affects\-switch, "issue", critical\)
- New window launched when a component receives focus\. \(focus\-window\-launches, "issue", serious\)
- When a component receives keyboard focus there is a significant change of content in the page \(above the user's point of regard\) that changes the meaning of the page\. \(unexpected\-change\-on\-focus, "issue", critical\)
- When a component receives screen reader focus there is a significant change of content in the page \(above the user's point of regard\) that changes the meaning of the page\. \(unexpected\-change\-on\-focus\-screen\-reader, "issue", critical\)
- When a component receives switch control focus there is a significant change of content in the page \(above the user's point of regard\) that changes the meaning of the page\. \(unexpected\-change\-on\-focus\-switch, "issue", critical\)

__3\.2\.2\.a Context Changes \(on Input\)__

__Overview__

- General \- Entering data into, or changing the setting of, a user interface component must not trigger a change of context, unless the user is given a warning ahead of time\.
- HTML \- Entering data into, or changing the setting of, a user interface component must not trigger a change of context, unless the user is given a warning ahead of time\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

__IMPORTANT:__

- Testing for this checkpoint can be accomplished using either the "Manual" methodology or the "Assistive Technology" methodology or a combination of both\.
- This checkpoint does NOT apply to the results of selecting a button or link or tabs in a tab control\.

1. Check that entering data into, or changing the setting of, a user interface component does not trigger a context change \(See "Things to think about" below for a definition of "context change"\.\)\. 
	1. Using only a keyboard, navigate to all form controls or interactive elements where a user can enter data or change a setting of the element\.
	2. Ensure that you do not encounter a context change when data is entered or a setting is changed, for example: 
		- You can enter text in a text field, select checkboxes, move through a set of radio buttons or select options, or toggle a switch or a toggle button, without triggering a context change\.
		- Leaving the last field does not submit the form and trigger a context change\.
2. Check that, if a context change occurs in step 1, visible, accessible text instructions explaining this behavior are provided prior to the form element or control that triggers the context change\.

__Things to think about__

- __Definition of "context change"\.__ A change of context is a change of: 
	- User agent
	- Viewport
	- Focus
	- Content that changes the meaning of the current screen
- __A change of CONTENT is NOT always a change of CONTEXT\.__ Changes in content, such as an expanding outline, opening a datepicker, dynamic menu, or a tab control do not necessarily change the context, unless they also change one of the above \(e\.g\., focus\)\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- A change of context occurs without warning when the user changes the setting of a user interface control\. \(unexpected\-change\-on\-interaction, "issue", critical\)
- A change of context occurs without warning while a user is using the keyboard to navigate a dropdown control, before actually selecting an option\. \(unexpected\-change\-select, "issue", critical\)
- A window is automatically launched without warning when the user inputs a value in a text field or selects a checkbox or radio button\. \(unexpected\-window\-launch, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

- Provide submit button on all forms \- Provide a submit button on forms, even if the form can be implicitly submitted\. \(form\-implicit\-submit, "best\-practice", moderate, August 28, 2017\)
- Indicate new window \- Links that open in a new window should indicate this in the link text\. However, this is not a WCAG 2\.0 AA requirement\. It is a WCAG 2\.0 AAA issue under SC 3\.2\.5\. \(link\-target\-not\-indicated, "best\-practice", moderate, April 16, 2013\)

__3\.1\.1\.a Default Page Language__

__Overview__

- General \- The language of the screen must be identified for assistive technology\.
- HTML \- The language of the screen must be identified for assistive technology\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. If the lang attribute or the value provided is invalid, the automated tests in axe Auditor \(axe\-core rules\) will create an issue\.
2. Looking at the source code of the page, confirm that the lang attribute value on the html element is valid for the language that the content of the page is written in\. See [Deque University > Language Codes [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://dequeuniversity.com/class/semantic-structure/language/language-codes)](https://dequeuniversity.com/class/semantic-structure/language/language-codes)for more information including common language codes\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

Follow the testing methodology for Desktop Web \- Manual\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- The value of the lang attribute used to indicate the primary language of the page is not a valid language code\. \(lang\-invalid, "issue", serious\)
- The value of the lang attribute used to indicate the primary language of the page is for the wrong language\. \(lang\-not\-accurate, "issue", serious\)
- The lang attribute used to indicate the primary language of the page is missing from the HTML element\. \(lang\-not\-defined, "issue", serious\)
- The primary language of the application is not identified and its content is not announced correctly by a screen reader\. \(lang\-not\-defined\-non\-web, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

__3\.1\.2\.a Language of Parts__

__Overview__

- General \- The language of sections of content that are different from the screen's default language must be identified for assistive technology\.
- HTML \- The language of sections of content that are different from the page’s default language must be identified for assistive technology\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Read the text of the page to identify passages or phrases that are in a language that is different from the language of the page\. 
	- __Exceptions:__ Proper names, technical terms, words of indeterminate language, and words or phrases that have become part of the vernacular of the immediately surrounding text\.
2. Inspect the code to ensure that any changes in language are properly marked using the lang attribute\. 
	- The lang attribute can be applied to block level elements \(DIV, H1, P, TABLE, etc\.\) as well as inline elements \(SPAN, A, STRONG, etc\.\) 
	- As a general rule, it is best to use the two\-letter language code\. Avoid region, script or other subtags except where they add useful distinguishing information\.
	- __Note:__ If you can confirm that the pronunciation is acceptable without the language markup, it does not need to be marked up\. For example, you can use Google Translate to listen to the pronunciation of a single word in a different language that appears on the page\. If you cannot determine the original language and therefore the correct pronunciation, you must create an issue\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

Follow the testing methodology for Desktop Web \- Manual\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- The change in language for a portion of content is not coded\. \(lang\-change\-not\-marked, "issue", serious\)
- The change in language for a portion of content is not correct\. \(lang\-change\-not\-valid, "issue", serious\)

__SC 2\.5\.8 Target Size \(Minimum\) \(Tamaño del Objetivo \- Mínimo\)__

__Descripción General__

Este criterio establece un __tamaño mínimo__ para los objetivos de puntero \(como botones, enlaces o áreas interactivas\) para que sean fáciles de seleccionar, especialmente en dispositivos táctiles o para usuarios con dificultades de control motor\. El tamaño mínimo recomendado es de __24x24 CSS pixels__, aunque hay excepciones y consideraciones adicionales\.

__¿A quién beneficia?__

1. __Personas con discapacidades motoras__: Aquellos que tienen dificultades para realizar movimientos precisos con el mouse, el teclado o dispositivos táctiles\.
2. __Personas que usan dispositivos táctiles__: Los dedos son menos precisos que un puntero de mouse, por lo que los objetivos más grandes son más fáciles de tocar\.
3. __Personas con baja visión__: Pueden tener dificultades para ver y seleccionar objetivos pequeños\.

__Requisitos clave__

1. __Tamaño mínimo del objetivo__:
	- Los objetivos de puntero deben tener al menos __24x24 CSS pixels__ de tamaño\.
	- Esto incluye el área visible del objetivo y cualquier espacio adicional que no interfiera con otros elementos\.
2. __Excepciones__:
	- __Objetivos equivalentes__: Si el mismo objetivo está disponible en otra parte de la página con un tamaño adecuado, no es necesario que cumpla con el tamaño mínimo\.
	- __Control de usuario__: Si el tamaño del objetivo está determinado por el usuario \(por ejemplo, un campo de búsqueda que se expande al hacer clic\), no es necesario que cumpla con el tamaño mínimo inicial\.
	- __Espacio limitado__: En casos donde el espacio es limitado \(por ejemplo, en una barra de herramientas\), se permite un tamaño menor, siempre que haya una alternativa accesible\.
3. __Espaciado entre objetivos__:
	- Si los objetivos están muy cerca entre sí \(menos de 24px de separación\), se debe proporcionar un mecanismo para evitar errores de selección \(por ejemplo, un modo de ampliación o confirmación\)

__2\.2\.2\.a Pause, Stop, or Hide Content__

__Overview__

- General \- Automatically moving, blinking, or scrolling content that is presented in parallel with other content and lasts longer than 5 seconds must be able to be paused, stopped, or hidden by the user\.
- HTML \- Automatically moving, blinking, or scrolling content that is presented in parallel with other content and lasts longer than 5 seconds must be able to be paused, stopped, or hidden by the user\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Identify moving, blinking, or scrolling information where the movement, blinking or scrolling lasts for more than 5 seconds\.
2. Verify that there is a mechanism available to allow the user to stop, pause, or hide the content\.
3. __Exceptions:__ 
	- When the moving, blinking, or scrolling content is NOT presented in parallel with other content, for example: a page loading indicator\.
	- When the movement, blinking, or scrolling is part of an activity where it is essential\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- Auto\-playing media or animation has no mechanism to pause, stop, or hide the content\. \(content\-auto\-play, "issue", serious\)
- The element uses a BLINK element or text\-decoration: blink\. \(content\-blink, "issue", serious\)
- A MARQUEE element is present\. \(content\-marquee, "issue", serious\)
- Moving or blinking content that lasts more than 5 seconds does not have a mechanism to pause, stop or hide it\. \(content\-motion\-uninterruptible, "issue", serious\)

__2\.2\.2\.b Automatically Updating Content__

__Overview__

- General \- Automatically updating content that is presented in parallel with other content must be able to be paused, stopped, or hidden by the user or the user can manually control the timing of the updates\.
- HTML \- Automatically updating content that is presented in parallel with other content must be able to be paused, stopped, or hidden by the user or the user can manually control the timing of the updates\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Identify all automatically updating content\.
2. Verify that there is a mechanism available to control the frequency of the update\.
3. __Exceptions:__ 
	- When the automatically updating content is NOT presented in parallel with other content, for example: a site requires that all users view a 15 second advertisement before they can access free content available from their site\.
	- When the auto\-updating is part of an activity where it is essential\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is required for this checkpoint\.__

__2\.2\.1\.a Timing Adjustable__

__Overview__

- General \- If a screen or application has a time limit, the user must be given options to turn off, adjust, or extend that time limit\.
- HTML \- If a page or application has a time limit, the user must be given options to turn off, adjust, or extend that time limit\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Identify content that has a time limit set by the website or application\. This includes session timeouts, time limits on filling out a form or completing an activity, messages that appear for a time and then disappear \(see note about "Toast Messages" below\), etc\.
2. Verify at least one of the following is true: 
	- The user can __turn off__ the time limit before encountering it\.
	- The user can __adjust__ the time limit before encountering it over a wide range that is at least ten times the length of the default setting\.
	- The user is __warned__ before time expires and given at least __20 seconds to extend the time limit__ with a simple action \(for example, "press the Space Bar"\), and the user can __extend the time limit at least 10 times__\.
3. Using a __keyboard alone__ ensure any mechanisms to turn off, adjust, or extend the time limit are keyboard accessible\.

__Exceptions__

- __Real\-time Exception:__ The time limit is a required part of a real\-time event and no alternative to the time limit is possible\. 
	- Example: a live auction\.
- __Essential Exception:__ The time limit is essential and extending it would invalidate the activity\. 
	- Example: The time limit on a One Time Passcode \(OTP\) in multi\-factor authentication\.
	- Example: An on\-line ticket\-purchasing site gives the user two minutes to confirm a purchase before the seats are returned to the general pool\. Because tickets on such sites can sell out quickly, holding a ticket longer than that may invalidate the nature of the site\.
- __20 Hour Exception:__ The time limit is longer than 20 hours\.

__Note: Not all "Toast Messages" are bad\!__

- If a temporarily displayed message requires user interaction or is a warning that the user needs to know about and it isn’t available on the same page or 1 page away once the message goes away \- that is a failure of this checkpoint\.
- If a temporarily displayed message is a confirmation message or notification or similar and the information is available \(on the same page or 1 page away\), that is not a failure of this checkpoint\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is required for this checkpoint\.__

Using a __screen reader__ follow the same procedure as Desktop Web\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- Content visually appears for a short time and then disappears\. The user has no way to adjust when the content visually appears or disappears\. Examples of content include error messages, success messages, and updating headlines\. \(content\-disappears, "issue", serious\)
- The mechanism to extend the session is not accessible by keyboard alone\. \(keyboard\-cannot\-extend\-session, "issue", critical\)
- The content time out warning does not give the user at least 20 seconds to extend the session with a simple action \(for example, "press the spacebar"\)\. \(timeout\-extension\-grace, "issue", serious\)
- Content times out without providing any warning to the user\. \(timeout\-no\-warning, "issue", critical\)
- Screen reader users are not made aware of the presence of the time out warning\. \(timeout\-not\-announced, "issue", critical\)
- The timeout warning does not provide a way to turn off the time limit or to adjust or extend the limit at least 10 times\. \(timeout\-not\-extensible, "issue", critical\)
- The mechanism to extend the session is not accessible by a screen reader\. \(timeout\-screenreader, "issue", critical\)
- The mechanism to extend the session is not accessible by switch control\. \(timeout\-switch, "issue", critical\)

__3\.3\.2\.a Visible Labels__

__Overview__

- General \- When a form field takes user input, visible labels must be provided to identify the field so users know what input data is expected\.
- HTML \- When a form field takes user input, visible labels must be provided to identify the field so users know what input data is expected\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Check that __form fields that require user input__ \(i\.e\. data entry and submission fields such as text boxes, radio buttons, checkboxes, submit buttons, etc\.\) have a visible label\.
2. Some special scenarios to think about: 
	- For fields whose label is inside a text or textarea input, type data into the field and check that the field label stays visible by "floating" to the top of the field or other method\.
	- For a group of fields used for one data point \(such as a phone number, social security number, or date\), check that there is a visual text label for sighted users to know the purpose of that group of fields\.
	- For a search input that does not have a visible label or only has placeholder text, check that there is a submit button or an icon for the field that indicates the field’s purpose such as a button labeled “search” or a magnifying glass icon\.
	- For a chat or IM input that does not have a visible label or only has placeholder text, check that there is a submit button for the field that indicates the field’s purpose such as a button labeled "send" or an "airplane" or other icon\.
	- For a single selection picker element, the first option can be used as a label as long as it isn't an option itself\. For example, State as the first option of a single selection picker passes, but Alabama does not\.
	- It is permissible for labels to appear only when a field is hovered and focused\. For example, the "Select all messages" checkbox on the Gmail Inbox screen\. Keep in mind, this may not be a viable solution for mobile web\.
	- A pulldown menu that limits the scope of a search is an exception to this requirement\.

__Things to think about__

- This checkpoint does not apply to links or other controls \(such as an expand/collapse widget, or similar interactive components\) that are not associated with data entry\.
- While this checkpoint requires that controls and inputs for data entry and submission have labels, whether or not these labels are sufficiently clear or descriptive is covered separately by checkpoint 2\.4\.6\.b Descriptive Labels\.
- An icon MAY serve as a visible label\. Examples of common icons that label form fields \(or user controls\) include: magnifying glass \(for search\), 3 horizontal lines on top of each other \(hamburger menu\), gear \(preferences or settings\), trash can \(delete or view trash depending on context\), "airplane" icon \(chat or IM\)\.
- If this checkpoint fails, keep 2\.5\.3 Label in Name in mind when suggesting a visible label\. The visible label and accessible name must correspond to pass 2\.5\.3\.
- Common Failures: 
	- __Placeholders alone as visual labels \(except for single select elements\)__ \- Placeholders cannot be used alone as the label because they vanish when you enter data in the text field\. Exception: In a single select element, a persistent placeholder label option as the first item in the list can be used for minimal compliance with WCAG 2\.0 SC 3\.3\.2\.
	- __Login/Password fields not visually labeled__ \- The login and password fields need visible labels\. It is not WCAG compliant to just use placeholders as labels in text fields\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

Screen reader testing is not applicable for this checkpoint

__Issue Descriptions__

- No visual group label is present and the purpose of this group is not clear without a visual label\. \(label\-group\-not\-present, "issue", serious\)
- No persistent visual label is present for a select or dropdown control and the purpose of this field is not clear without a visual label\. \(label\-is\-missing\-select, "issue", moderate\)
- Label is not persistent\. For example: placeholder is being used as the only visual label for a text field\. \(label\-is\-placeholder, "issue", serious\)
- Chat or IM input label is not persistent\. \(label\-is\-placeholder\-chat, "issue", minor\)
- No visual label is present and the purpose of this field is not clear without a visual label\. \(label\-not\-present, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

- Text label in close proximity \- Position the text label in close proximity to the associated field to assist users of screen magnifiers because the field and label are more likely to both be visible within the magnified area of the page\. \(label\-position\-bad, "best\-practice", serious, March 5, 2018\)
- Label always visible for single select \- Provide a label for the single select element that is always visible, even when a selection is made and the single select element is collapsed\. \(label\-select\-placeholder, "best\-practice", moderate, June 12, 2017\)

__3\.3\.2\.b Missing Instructions__

__Overview__

- General \- When a form field takes user input, sufficient labels or instructions must be provided so users know what input data is expected\.
- HTML \- When a form field takes user input, sufficient labels or instructions must be provided so users know what input data is expected\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

Testing for this checkpoint can be accomplished using either the "Manual" methodology or the "Assistive Technology" methodology or a combination of both\.

1. Check that when __form fields require user input__ \(i\.e\. data entry and submission fields such as text inputs, radio buttons, checkboxes, submit buttons, etc\.\) essential labels or instructions are available __in text__ or are available as some __other visible component with a text alternative__\.
2. Verify that AT LEAST ONE of the following is true: 
	- Essential text labels or instructions are available for the form, set of fields, or a field\.
	- If a label or instruction is provided by a non\-text element \(e\.g\. an image\), the non\-text content has an accurate text alternative\.
	- A text error message is provided that helps users complete the form correctly\.
3. Verify that a screen reader can read the instructions or error message\. Make sure the instructions or error message are consistent for both sighted and screen reader users\.

__Things to think about__

- This checkpoint does not apply to links or other controls \(such as an expand/collapse widget, or other interactive components\) that are not associated with data entry\.
- WCAG SC 3\.3\.2 does __NOT__ require programmatic association of essential instructions\.
- WCAG SC 3\.3\.2 does __NOT__ require field level instructions for data formats or valid ranges\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

1. Check that when __form fields require user input__ \(i\.e\. data entry and submission fields such as text inputs, radio buttons, checkboxes, submit buttons, etc\.\) essential labels or instructions are available __in text__ or are available as some __other visible component with a text alternative__\.
2. Verify that AT LEAST ONE of the following is true: 
	- Essential text labels or instructions are available for the form, set of fields, or a field\.
	- If a label or instruction is provided by a non\-text element \(e\.g\. an image\), the non\-text content has an accurate text alternative\.
	- A text error message is provided that helps users complete the form correctly\.

__Things to think about__

- This checkpoint does not apply to links or other controls \(such as an expand/collapse widget, or other interactive components\) that are not associated with data entry\.
- WCAG SC 3\.3\.2 does __NOT__ require programmatic association of essential instructions\.
- WCAG SC 3\.3\.2 does __NOT__ require field level instructions for data formats or valid ranges\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- Additional instructions are only provided for the input field to people who do not have disabilities\. For a person with disabilities, the instructions are incomplete, inaccurate, or misleading\. \(instructions\-not\-accurate, "issue", serious\)
- Additional instructions are needed for the input field but are not provided for people with disabilities\. People without disabilities have access to additional instructions\. \(instructions\-not\-included, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

- Provide format instructions \- Labels or instructions should specify when an input field requires a specific format\. \(input\-format\-clarification, "best\-practice", moderate, August 29, 2016\)
- Label should convey range of valid values \- When an input is limited to a range of values, the label or instructions should convey this\. \(input\-range\-clarification, "best\-practice", moderate, March 27, 2017\)
- Programmatically assoc instructions/descriptions \- When additional instructions/descriptions are provided for a control, they should be programmatically associated with the control\. \(instructions\-not\-associated, "best\-practice", serious, March 27, 2017\)
- Descriptive instructions \- Instructions for controls should be clear and sufficiently descriptive\. \(instructions\-not\-descriptive, "best\-practice", moderate, June 27, 2016\)
- Position labels and instructions near related element \- Position labels and instructions near their corresponding element so it is easy to visually associate them with the related element, especially when zooming or magnifying the screen\. \(instructions\-positioned\-improperly, "best\-practice", serious, November 4, 2016\)

__3\.3\.2\.c Required Form Fields__

__Overview__

- General \- Required form fields must be indicated by a field label, instructions, or error message\.
- HTML \- Required form fields must be indicated by a field label, instructions, or error message\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

Testing for this checkpoint can be accomplished using either the "Manual" methodology or the "Assistive Technology" methodology or a combination of both\.

1. Locate forms in the content\.
2. Fill out the form, deliberately leaving required fields blank, and submit the form\.
3. For each required form field verify that AT LEAST ONE of the following is true: 
	- The field label or group label \(e\.g\. legend for radio buttons\) indicates which fields are required\.
	- Text instructions at the beginning of the form indicate which fields are required \(or optional\)\.
	- An error message indicates which required field/fields was/were not completed\.

__Things to think about__

- Using an asterisk \(\*\) as the only visual indicator of a required field minimally passes this checkpoint\. It would be much better to also include a note at the top of the form similar to "Required fields are marked with \*"
- A form that has a single field that needs input with no visual indication that it is required AND has a submit button that is disabled until data is entered into the field PASSES this checkpoint\. That is because the fact that the form only has one field that needs input indicates by default that it is required; therefore, it does not need to otherwise meet the three points above\. 
- A form that has multiple fields that need input with no visual indication of required fields AND has a submit button that is disabled until all required fields are filled FAILS this checkpoint\. That is because the third option \- an error message \- cannot be triggered\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

1. Locate forms in the content\.
2. Fill out the form, deliberately leaving required fields blank, and submit the form\.
3. For each required form field verify that AT LEAST ONE of the following is true: 
	- The field label or group label \(e\.g\. legend for radio buttons\) indicates which fields are required\.
	- Text instructions at the beginning of the form indicate which fields are required \(or optional\)\.
	- An error message is provided that indicates which required field/fields was/were not completed\.

- __Notes:__ 
	- Using an asterisk \(\*\) as the only visual indicator of a required field minimally passes this checkpoint\. It would be much better to also include a note at the top of the form similar to "Required fields are marked with \*"
	- A form that has a single field that needs input with no visual indication that it is required AND has a submit button that is disabled until data is entered into the field PASSES this checkpoint\. That is because the fact that the form only has one field that needs input indicates by default that it is required; therefore, it does not need to otherwise meet the three points above\. 
	- A form that has multiple fields that need input with no visual indication of required fields AND has a submit button that is disabled until all required fields are filled FAILS this checkpoint\. That is because the third option \- an error message \- cannot be triggered\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- The required field is not identified by any of the following means: its label or form\-level instructions or an error message\. \(form\-error\-required\-unclear, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

- Markup each required field with aria\-required="true" \- Required fields should be marked with aria\-required="true" \(aria\-required\-missing, "best\-practice", moderate, January 9, 2017\)
- Provide instruction when using \* to indicate required fields \- When an asterisk is used to indicate required form fields, provide an instruction above the form such as "Fields marked with \* are required\." \(asterisk\-instruction\-missing, "best\-practice", moderate, January 14, 2022\)
- Avoid marking optional field as required \- Optional fields should not be marked as required to successfully submit the form\. \(optional\-marked\-required, "best\-practice", moderate, August 22, 2016\)

__1\.3\.5\.a Identify Input Purpose__

__Overview__

- General \- The purpose for each common input field that collects an individual's personal data must be programmatically defined based on the list of 53 Input Purposes for User Interface Components\.
- HTML \- The purpose for each common input field that collects an individual's personal data must be programmatically defined based on the list of 53 Input Purposes for User Interface Components\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Identify any form elements that accept an individual's personal data\.
2. For each form element that accepts an individual's personal data: 
	- Check to see if the personal data is one of the 53 types identified in WCAG 2\.1 Section 7 [Input Purposes for User Interface Components [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/#input-purposes)](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/#input-purposes)\.
	- Confirm that the autocomplete attribute is set with the appropriate value to identify the purpose of that UI component\.

__Things to think about__

- Elements that support the autocomplete attribute include: 
	- input with a type of date, email, hidden, month, number, password, search, tel, text, OR url
	- textarea
	- select
- The ARIA equivalents to HTML elements such as role="combobox" do not require autocomplete attributes\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

Follow the testing methodology for Desktop Web \- Manual\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- Autocomplete attribute to identify the purpose of this form element is not accurate\. \(id\-input\-purpose\-inaccurate, "issue", serious\)
- Autocomplete attribute to identify the purpose of this form element is missing\. \(id\-input\-purpose\-missing, "issue", serious\)

__Autocomplete Attribute Tokens that can be used to meet WCAG 2\.1 SC 1\.3\.5 Identify Input Purpose__

See [Input Purposes for User Interface Components [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG21/#input-purposes)](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG21/#input-purposes)for definitions of these terms\.

