__1\.3\.3\.a Visual Cues__

__Overview__

- General \- Instructions provided to users must rely on more than just visual cues \(characteristics such as shape, color, size, visual location, or orientation\)\.
- HTML \- Instructions provided to users must rely on more than just visual cues \(characteristics such as shape, color, size, visual location, or orientation\)\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Review the page to identify any instructions related to understanding content or operating content\.
2. Determine if any of those instructions reference visual characteristics such as shape, color, size, visual location, or orientation\.
3. For any instructions that reference visual characteristics, check that they also reference another non\-visual characteristic \(e\.g\.: a text label which can be accessed by a screen reader and by people who cannot perceive color\)\.

__Reality check:__ None of the instructions on the page rely on my ability to see things such as shape, color, size, visual location, or orientation\.

__Note:__ In some languages, it is commonly understood that "above" refers to the content previous to that point in the content and "below" refers to the content after that point\. In such languages, if the content being referenced is in the appropriate place in the reading order and the references are unambiguous, statements such as "choose one of the links below" or "all of the above" would pass this checkpoint\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

Follow the testing methodology for Desktop Web \- Manual\.

__Examples__

If a round button \(labeled with the text "go"\) is used to advance to the next step in a web process, then accessible instructions would be, "to advance, press the round button labeled go"\. This includes both shape and textual information to locate the button\. 

__Issue Descriptions__

- Instructions are presented in a way that requires the ability to see shape, color, size, visual location, or orientation\. \(information\-requires\-vision, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

__1\.3\.3\.b Sound Cues__

__Overview__

- General \- Instructions provided to users must rely on more than just sound or auditory cues\.
- HTML \- Instructions provided to users must rely on more than just sound or auditory cues\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Hearing or hearing assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Review the content to identify any instructions related to understanding content or operating content\.
2. Determine if any of those instructions reference sound prompts\.
3. For any instructions that reference sound prompts, check that they also reference another non\-sound characteristic that can be accessed by people who cannot hear sound\.

__Reality Check:__ There are no instructions that rely solely on my ability to hear\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

Follow the testing methodology for Desktop Web \- Manual\.

__Examples__

If a multiple screen process required the user to hear a sound cue before being allowed to move on to the next screen using the right arrow key, this indication would also be provided visually in text, so the process is not only depending on the ability to hear sounds\. 

__Issue Descriptions__

- Instructions are presented in a way that requires the ability to hear sound, and there is no alternate method to convey the information\. \(information\-requires\-hearing, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

2 month

__2\.5\.1\.a Pointer Gestures__

__Overview__

- General \- All functionality that can be operated with a pointer must be operable with single\-pointer actions\. Path\-based or multi\-point gestures are not required to operate any functionality\. Exceptions exist\. 
- HTML \- All functionality that can be operated with a pointer must be operable with single\-pointer actions\. Path\-based or multi\-point gestures are not required to operate any functionality\. Exceptions exist\. 

__Testing Methodology__

1. Identify any functionality that can be triggered by: 
	- Path\-based gestures \(see description of path\-based gestures below\) OR
	- Multipoint gestures \(see description of multipoint gestures below\)
	- Note 1: This checkpoint does NOT apply to functionality/gestures that are part of the operating system, user agent or assistive technology\.
	- Note 2: This checkpoint does not require all functionality to be available through pointing devices\. It does require that any functionality that is available using complex pointer gestures \(path\-based or multipoint\) also be operable by single\-pointer actions that are not path\-based\. 
2. For each functionality that can be triggered by path\-based gestures or multipoint gestures, verify that the functionality can also be operated with the use of single\-pointer actions that are not path\-based \(such as taps, double taps, long presses, clicks, click and holds, double clicks, or dragging actions that are not path\-based\)\. 
3. __Exception:__ 
	- __Essential:__ Path\-based gestures and/or multipoint gestures are essential for the function and replacing them would invalidate the activity\. For example, entering one's signature is inherently path\-based \(although acknowledging something or confirming one's identity is not\)\.

__Path\-based gesture:__ A path\-based gesture involves an interaction where not just the endpoints matter\. If going through an intermediate point \(usually near the start of the gesture\) also affects its meaning then it is a path\-based gesture\. The user engages a pointer \(starting point\), carries out a movement that goes through at least one intermediate\-point before disengaging the pointer \(end point\)\. The intermediate point defines the gesture as requiring a specific path, even if the complete path is not defined\. Pointer interactions where the movement between the start and end points can go in any direction and are entirely non\-prescriptive are not path\-based\.

__Multipoint gesture:__ Includes a two\-finger pinch zoom, a split tap where one finger rests on the screen and a second finger taps, or a two\- or three\-finger tap or swipe\.

__Examples:__

Pointer Gestures \(in scope for this checkpoint\):

- Weather app: Swipe horizontally to see additional hours or days in a forecast\.
- Product carousel: Swipe horizontally to see additional products\.
- Map app: Use pinch\-to\-zoom to zoom in or zoom out on the map\.

Dragging \(in scope for checkpoint 2\.5\.7\.a Dragging Movements, if doing a WCAG 2\.2 Level AA assessment\):

- Slider: Drag the slider "thumb" along the slider track\.
- Sort or reorder items: Drag items from one position to another in a list or between locations\.
- Kanban board: Drag horizontally or vertically to move an item to a different position within a column or to a different column\.
- Draw shapes: Draw or change the size of a rectangle \(or other shape\) by down\-clicking on a corner or handle of the shape and dragging to a new size\.
- Drawing lines or connections: Draw a line between two points or two objects \(such as a matching test\) by down\-clicking in one place and dragging the pointer to another place\.

__1\.3\.4\.a Device Orientation__

__Overview__

- General \- Orientation of the content must not be locked to either landscape or portrait, unless a specific orientation is essential for the functionality\.
- HTML \- Orientation of the content must not be locked to either landscape or portrait, unless a specific orientation is essential for the functionality\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

__Important:__ The testing methodology for 1\.3\.4\.a Orientation below uses a responsive simulation in a desktop Chrome browser\. Testing via simulation vs on a mobile device does not guarantee 100% accuracy\.

1. Ensure the browser zoom is set to 100%\.
2. Open Chrome > Developer Tools > Inspect panel\.
3. Select "Toggle device toolbar"\.
4. Select "Responsive" from the Dimensions dropdown menu\.
5. Set the dimensions to 1366 x 768 px \(landscape orientation\)\. It is OK if the zoom percentage automatically changes to accommodate the screen dimensions\.
6. Use the Rotate button to simulate rotating the screen to 768 x 1366 px \(portrait orientation\)\. Again, it is OK if the zoom percentage automatically changes to accommodate the screen dimensions\.
7. Ensure both of the following are true: 
	- The visual display of the content adjusts to the new orientation\.
	- All content and functionality that were available in the original orientation are still available somewhere within the web site in the second orientation \(not necessarily on the same screen\)\.
8. Repeat the above steps starting with the portrait orientation and rotating to landscape orientation\.

OPTIONAL \- Test with a mobile device:

1. Make sure you have UNLOCKED the device orientation setting at the operating system level\.
2. Open the webpage while holding your device in portrait orientation\.
3. Ensure the page automatically displays in the mode you’re holding your device\.
4. Rotate the mobile device to the landscape orientation\. Ensure both of the following are true: 
	- The visual display of the content adjusts to the landscape orientation\.
	- All content and functionality that were available in portrait orientation are still available somewhere within the web site in landscape orientation \(not necessarily on the same screen\)\.
5. Repeat the above steps starting with landscape orientation and rotating to portrait orientation\.

__Things to think about__

- __Exception: Essential__ \- A screen doesn’t fail this checkpoint if the functionality of a particular feature requires a specific orientation such as a piano keyboard application\. Note that this exception only applies if there is no other way to perform the function or present the information\. Steps should always be taken to ensure this exception isn’t necessary\.
- How much content is visible on a screen may change at different screen orientations due to responsive design changes\. For example, navigation menus that are fully visible in a landscape orientation may be collapsed in a portrait orientation into a single menu button \(the 'hamburger' icon pattern\) or sections of a screen may be collapsed under toggled controls so they take up less screen space\. This checkpoint is passed __as long as__ all content and functionality are still available either: directly on the screen, revealed via accessible controls on the same screen, accessible via direct links from the screen being assessed, or via clear instructions on how to locate the content or functionality\.

__3\.2\.3\.a Consistent Navigation Patterns__

__Overview__

- General \- Navigation patterns that are repeated across multiple screens must be presented in the same relative order each time they appear\.
- HTML \- Navigation patterns that are repeated across multiple screens must be presented in the same relative order each time they appear\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

__Review the webpages within a site, a logical section of a site, or a web application:__

1. Look for navigation components \(such as navigation menus, search fields or tables of contents\) that are repeated from one page to another\. 
2. Verify that the main navigation links are in the same relative order on each page\.
3. If there are multiple navigation components, verify that the components are in the same relative place and order on each page\.

__Reality Check:__ The order of repeated navigation aids, such as menus, menu items and search fields, are the same on each page so I don't have to relearn the order and location as I navigate through a site\. 

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- Navigational elements that occur across multiple pages or screens are not presented in the same relative order\. \(inconsistent\-nav, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

__3\.2\.4\.a Consistent Identification__

__Overview__

- General \- Components that have the same functionality across multiple screens must be consistently identified\.
- HTML \- Components that have the same functionality across multiple screens must be consistently identified\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

Testing for this checkpoint can be accomplished using either the "Manual" methodology or the "Assistive Technology" methodology or a combination of both\.

1. Review the webpages within a site, a logical section of a site, or a web application\.
2. Look for user interface elements \(such as links, buttons, active icons, informational icons, objects, etc\.\) that are repeated across webpages\.
3. Use Chrome > Inspect > Elements > Accessibility tab \(this tab may be hidden behind the More Tabs >> button\) > Computed Properties to inspect the accessible Name of the element\.
4. Verify that each element that shares the same function and is repeated across multiple pages is identified using a consistent label, name or text alternative\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

1. Review the webpages within a site, a logical section of a site, or a web application\.
2. Look for user interface elements \(such as links, buttons, active icons, informational icons, objects, etc\.\) that are repeated across webpages\.
3. With a screen reader turned on: for each repeated control, verify that the label voiced by the screen reader is consistent\.

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- Components are labeled differently on different pages or screens, though they have the same function\. \(inconsistent\-component\-id, "issue", serious\)
- The form control is identified differently on different pages or screens, though it has the same function\. \(inconsistent\-form\-id, "issue", serious\)
- The link/image link is identified differently on different pages, though it has the same target\. \(inconsistent\-link, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

__3\.2\.6\.a Consistent Help__

__Overview__

- General \- If a page or screen contains a help mechanism which is repeated on multiple pages or screens within a set of pages or screens, the mechanism must occur in the same relative order to other content, unless a change is initiated by the user\.
- HTML \- If a page or screen contains a help mechanism which is repeated on multiple pages or screens within a set of pages or screens, the mechanism must occur in the same relative order to other content, unless a change is initiated by the user\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

__Note:__ This checkpoint does not require that help be provided\. The checkpoint requires that IF help is available on multiple screens, it is in a consistent __programmatic__ location, for example, in the same relative code order within a page area such as the header or footer\.

1. Identify __IF__ any of the following help mechanisms, or links to the help mechanism, are provided on a page\. If no help mechanisms exist, this checkpoint is Not Applicable\. 
	- Human contact details \(examples: phone number or email address\)
	- Human contact mechanism \(example: chat with a customer service person\)
	- Self\-help option \(example: Frequently Asked Questions page\)
	- A fully automated contact mechanism \(example: automated chat bot\)
2. __IF__ any of the above help mechanisms are provided on the page being assessed, determine if any of them appear in other pages within the set of pages being assessed\. __If a help mechanism is dropped or all help mechanisms are dropped from a page within a set, that does not fail this Success Criterion\.__ If the mechanism is only on the page being assessed, this checkpoint is Not Applicable\.
3. __IF__ any of the mechanisms do appear on other pages within the set of pages, ensure that each mechanism is provided in the same relative programmatic order \(such as the header or footer\) on each of the pages that contain the mechanism\.

__Things to think about:__

- This Success Criterion is scoped to "a set of pages"\. This is defined as: "a collection of web pages that share a common purpose and that are created by the same author, group or organization\." Examples of "a set of pages" include: 
	- A workflow such as a job application, shopping cart, or insurance application workflow\.
	- The "Investor Relations", "Newsroom", or "Account Services" portion of a larger website\.
- This Success Criterion requires that help be in the same relative location __in the code__\. Its visual location may vary from page\-to\-page as long as it is in the same relative __programmatic__ location from page\-to\-page\. Examples of same relative programmatic location include: in the header, in the footer, in a side navigation area\.
- The __programmatic__ help location may change as the viewport size changes, but it must be consistent across pages at the same viewport size\.

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- Help mechanism is not in a programmatically consistent location\. \(help\-not\-consistent, "issue", moderate\)

__Best Practices__

- All the same help mechanisms are available on all pages in the set \- All the same help mechanisms are available on all pages in the set\. \(help\-all\-always\-available, "best\-practice", moderate, November 3, 2022\)
- Help is available on every page in the set \- Every page in the set contains some form of help mechanism\. \(help\-always\-available, "best\-practice", moderate, November 3, 2022\)
- Help location is consistent throughout entire site \- Help is consistently located throughout the entire site \(even across different sets of pages\)\. \(help\-consistent\-every\-page, "best\-practice", minor, November 3, 2022\)
- Information on help availability is provided \- If a help mechanism is not always available, information about its availability \(such as hours of availability\) is provided\. \(help\-time\-described, "best\-practice", moderate, November 3, 2022\)
- Help is in the same visual and programmatic location \- Help mechanism is available in both the same visual and programmatic location\. \(help\-visually\-consistent, "best\-practice", moderate, November 3, 2022\)

__2\.4\.5\.a Multiple Ways__

__Overview__

- General \- Multiple ways must be available to find other screens in the application \- at least two of: links between pages, navigation menus, site search, site map, table of contents, or a list of related pages\.
- HTML \- Multiple ways must be available to find other screens in the application \- at least two of: links between pages, navigation menus, site search, site map, table of contents, or a list of related pages\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. This checkpoint is not applicable if one or more of the following is true: 
	- The page is not within a set of related web pages, OR
	- The webpage is the result of, or a step in, a process\.
2. Otherwise, verify that there are two or more ways to locate the specific web page within a set of web pages\. These ways can include \(but are not limited to\): 
	- Links between pages
	- Navigation menus
	- Site search
	- Site map
	- Table of contents
	- List of related pages

__Note:__ For a three or four page site, with all pages linked from the home page, it may be sufficient simply to provide links from and to the home page\.

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- Fewer than two ways to find other pages on the site are provided\. \(structure\-finding\-pages\-difficult, "issue", moderate\)
- Fewer than two ways to find other screens within a set of software programs are provided\. \(structure\-finding\-screens\-difficult, "issue", moderate\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

__3\.3\.7\.a Redundant Entry__

__Overview__

- General \- A multi\-step process must not require a user to re\-enter information that they have already entered or that was provided to them during the process\. Exceptions exist\.
- HTML \- A multi\-step process must not require a user to re\-enter information that they have already entered or that was provided to them during the process\. Exceptions exist\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Identify any processes that require a user to enter data\.
2. Examine the entire process to ensure that the user is not asked to enter the same data twice\.
3. If the user is asked to enter the same data twice, determine if there is a mechanism to support the re\-entry such as: 
	- The data is auto\-populated by the application, OR
	- The data is available to select so that it doesn’t need to be retyped, for example it can be: 
		- Selected from a drop down or via a radio button or checkbox
		- Selected, copied and pasted from text on the same screen
4. If no support mechanism exists, determine whether one of the following exceptions applies: 
	- __Re\-entering the information is essential\.__ For example, a memory game would be invalidated if the previous answers were supplied\.
	- __Ensuring the security of content\.__ For example, asking users to re\-enter a password while creating a new password in order to confirm the user typed what they thought they typed\.
	- __Information is no longer valid\.__ For example, when the previously entered information is no longer valid, requesting that the user enter that information again is allowed\.

__Things to think about__

- This Success Criterion applies to a single process\. 
	- This Success Criterion does not add a requirement for the web content / application to remember information between sessions, i\.e\. when a user returns after closing a session or navigating away\.
	- A process can run across different domains, so if a check\-out process includes a 3rd party payment provider, that would be in scope\.
- "Available to select" includes allowing the user to: 
	- Select and populate a field, including from a drop\-down
	- Select text from the same page and copy it into an input
	- Tick a checkbox to populate inputs with the same values as previously entered \(e\.g\., my billing address is the same as my shipping address\)\.
- This checkpoint does not apply if data is provided by the user through a different method, such as uploading a resume in a document format\.

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- Process requires a user to re\-enter information\. \(redundant\-entry\-required, "issue", moderate\)

__Best Practices__

- Provide a feature to show/hide a password \- Provide a feature to show/hide a password that the user has typed into a password field\. \(auth\-show\-password, "best\-practice", moderate, November 3, 2022\)

__3\.3\.8\.a Accessible Authentication \(Minimum\)__

__Overview__

- General \- Authentication processes must not rely on cognitive function tests UNLESS a mechanism is available to assist the user in completing the cognitive function test\. Exception: When the cognitive function test is to recognize common objects or content the user provided to the website\.
- HTML \- Authentication processes must not rely on cognitive function tests UNLESS a mechanism is available to assist the user in completing the cognitive function test\. Exception: When the cognitive function test is to recognize common objects or content the user provided to the website\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

Based on the client questionnaire, for __each__ step of __each__ authentication process flow, ensure that ONE of the following is true:

- There is at least one method or path available that does not rely on or include a cognitive function test in the flow \(see examples of cognitive function tests and exceptions below\)\.
- A mechanism is available to assist the user in completing the cognitive function test \(see examples of mechanisms below\)\.

__Things to think about__

- This requirement applies to all steps in the authentication process including: 
	- Multi\-factor authentication steps
	- Steps to recover an account or change the username or password
		- A __cognitive function test__ is a task that requires the user to remember, manipulate, or transcribe information\. Examples include, but are not limited to: 
			- Memorization, such as remembering a username, password, set of characters, images, or patterns 
				- __Exceptions:__ The common identifiers name, e\-mail, and phone number are not considered cognitive function tests as they are personal to the user and consistent across websites\.
			- Transcription, such as typing in characters from a one time passcode \(OTP\) provided by the site
			- Use of correct spelling
			- Performance of calculations
			- Solving of puzzles
			- CAPTCHAs 
				- __Exception:__ CAPTCHAs that use recognizing common objects pass this AA\-level Success Criterion\.
		- More on CAPTCHAs: 
			- Not all CAPTCHAs are in scope for this checkpoint\. Only CAPTCHAs that are found __during an authentication process__ are in scope\.
			- CAPTCHAs that require the user to type letters or numbers that they see on the screen fail this checkpoint\.
			- CAPTCHAs that ask people to select all the pictures that contain \[some common object\] pass this checkpoint\.
		- Examples of methods that assist the user in completing a cognitive function test include, but are not limited to: 
			- Support for \(don't not block\) password entry by browsers or password managers to address the memorization cognitive function test
			- Support for \(don't not block\) copy and paste to help address the transcription cognitive function test\.

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- Authentication process requires the completion of a non\-exempted cognitive function test and no mechanism is available to assist the user in completing the cognitive function test\. \(auth\-requires\-cognitive\-function, "issue", critical\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

__2\.3\.1\.a Flashing Content__

__Overview__

- General \- A screen must not contain content that flashes more than 3 times per second unless that flashing content is sufficiently small, and the flashes are of low contrast and do not exceed general flash thresholds\.
- HTML \- A page must not contain content that flashes more than 3 times per second unless that flashing content is sufficiently small, and the flashes are of low contrast and do not exceed general flash thresholds\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Inspect the screen or video for any flashing or blinking content\.
2. For each piece flashing or blinking content, count the number of times the content flashes or blinks in any one\-second period, or count the number of flashes or blinks in 10 seconds, and divide by 10\.
3. If the item flashes or blinks 3 or fewer times per second this checkpoint is passed\.
4. If the item flashes or blinks more than 3 times per second, then additional testing must happen to determine if the flashing passes the exception to this Success Criterion\. The exception allows flashing content to pass if the relative size of the flashing area is small enough \(i\.e\. less than 25% of 10 degrees of visual field\)\. This testing must be done with a tool such as [Photosensitive Epilepsy Analysis Tool \(PEAT\) [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://trace.umd.edu/peat#download)](https://trace.umd.edu/peat#download)on a Windows operating system \(free\) or [HardingFPA service [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.hardingfpa.com/)](https://www.hardingfpa.com/)\(for a fee\)\.

The [PEAT user guide [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://trace.umd.edu/photosensitive-epilepsy-analysis-tool-peat-user-guide/)](https://trace.umd.edu/photosensitive-epilepsy-analysis-tool-peat-user-guide/)provides complete instructions to run the tool \(only available on the Windows operating system\)\. An overview of steps follows:

1. Run the PEAT application\.
2. Open the web page to be tested in Firefox on the Windows 10 PC\. Note: \\You must use Firefox\.
3. In PEAT, select __Start __[__Capture [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://trace.umd.edu/peat/help#iCapturing_a_Video)__](https://trace.umd.edu/peat/help#iCapturing_a_Video)from the Capture Menu\.
4. In PEAT, play the video you just captured to make sure it recorded correctly\. Press the Play Button \(green button with black triangle\)\.
5. In PEAT, select [__Analyze [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://trace.umd.edu/peat/help#iAnalyzing_a_video)__](https://trace.umd.edu/peat/help#iAnalyzing_a_video)__Video__ from the Analysis Menu
6. Review the [PEAT Test Results and Analysis [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://trace.umd.edu/peat/help#iUnderstanding_test_results)](https://trace.umd.edu/peat/help#iUnderstanding_test_results)\.

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- Content flashes more than 3 times per second and exceeds 25% of 10 degrees of visual field in area\. \(content\-flashing\-big, "issue", blocker\)

__Best Practices__

- Avoid flashing content \- When possible, content should not flash at all\. \(content\-flashing, "best\-practice", serious, April 9, 2013\)

__2\.1\.4\.a Character Key Shortcuts__

__Overview__

- General \- If a single character key shortcut exists, then at least one of the following must be true: single character key shortcuts can be turned off, remapped or are only active when the relevant user interface component is in focus\.
- HTML \- If a single character key shortcut exists, then at least one of the following must be true: single character key shortcuts can be turned off, remapped or are only active when the relevant user interface component is in focus\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. __Ask the Developer:__ Because single character shortcuts can be difficult to discover, it is best to ask the developers of this page if they implemented any functionality that can be triggered by a single character shortcut\. Document this question and answer in writing for this assessment\.
2. Open the web page in a browser\.
3. Check the online Help documentation for keyboard shortcuts\. Try a few common Help key commands including: press and hold down the "shift" key, and press the "?" key to bring up a list of character key shortcuts\. If that does not work, also try this: press and hold down the "control" key, and press the "?" key to try to bring up a list of character key shortcuts\. While Shift\+? or Control\+? are commonly used, a developer may have chosen a different method for listing keyboard shortcuts\. Or there may not be any keyboard shortcuts at all\. See step 1: "Ask the Developer" 
	- __Pro Tip: __Remember to check for character key shortcuts on every page you test\. Do not assume that the same character key shortcuts are used across different pages within a web site/app\. 
4. Review the list of shortcuts and determine if any shortcuts can be triggered by typing only one printable character \(upper\-case letter, lower case letter, punctuation, number, or symbol character\)\.
5. If a single character key shortcut exists, determine if one of the following is true: 
	- __Turn off:__ a mechanism is available to turn\-off all the single character key shortcuts\. 
	- __Remap:__ a mechanism is available to remap all the single character key shortcuts to use one or more non\-printable keyboard keys \(like "Ctrl", "Alt", "Command", etc\.\) 
	- __Active Only on Focus:__ the single character key shortcut\(s\) are only active when the relevant user interface component has keyboard focus\. 

__Reality Check:__ If a Dragon Naturally Speaking user was focused on a text field on this web page, they could speak/input any single printable character and not trigger any shortcuts by mistake\. 

__Example:__ Gmail has single character key shortcuts that can be turned off\. See [How to Turn Gmail Keyboard Shortcuts On and Off [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://support.google.com/mail/answer/6594?hl=en)](https://support.google.com/mail/answer/6594?hl=en)\. There is also an experimental Gmail "lab" feature that gives users the ability to remap the Gmail character key shortcuts\.

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- Single character key shortcuts exist and do not have at least one of these required options: ability to turn off single character key shortcuts, ability to remap to a non\-singular key shortcut, OR are only active when the appropriate user interface component has focus\. \(keyboard\-character\-key\-shortcuts, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

__2\.5\.2\.a Pointer Cancellation__

__Overview__

- General \- For functionality that can be operated using a single\-pointer at least one of the following is true: no down\-event, abort/undo, up reversal or essential\.
- HTML \- For functionality that can be operated using a single\-pointer at least one of the following is true: no down\-event, abort/undo, up reversal or essential\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Find all controls on the page with actions that cannot be undone\.
2. If that control \(with an action that cannot be undone\) can be triggered by a single\-pointer touch, make sure that at least ONE of the following is true: 
	- __No Down\-Event__: The action is not triggered on the down event\.
	- __Abort or Undo__: The action triggers on the up event and a mechanism is available to abort the function before completion or to undo the function after completion\.
	- __Up Reversal__: The up\-event reverses any outcome of the preceding down\-event\.

__Essential Exception__: The nature of the action must be triggered on the "pointer down" event\. Examples: An onscreen piano keyboard, where the down event plays the appropriate note; an onscreen keyboard emulator, where the down event types a character; a game such as shooting skeets, where waiting for the "up" event would invalidate the precise timing necessary for the activation\.

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- Control submits an irreversible action on the down event\. \(touch\-down\-event, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

__2\.5\.4\.a Motion Actuation__

__Overview__

- General \- For functionality that can be triggered by a\) device motion \(like shaking, tilting\) or b\) user gestures detected by device sensors \(including a camera\), both of the following must be true: 1\) motion actuation can be disabled, and 2\) the functionality can be operated without using device motion or user gestures\. Exceptions exist\.
- HTML \- For functionality that can be triggered by a\) device motion \(like shaking, tilting\) or b\) user gestures detected by device sensors \(including a camera\), both of the following must be true: 1\) motion actuation can be disabled, and 2\) the functionality can be operated without using device motion or user gestures\. Exceptions exist\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. This checkpoint requires the use of a device that has motion sensors\. You must use a tablet or mobile device with motion sensors to test for this requirement\.
2. Identify any functionality that can be triggered by 
	- device motion \(such as shaking or tilting the device\) OR 
	- user motion detected by a device\. 
	- __Note: Motion Actuation does NOT cover__ 
		- the movement of users through space as registered by geolocation sensors or beacons\. 
		- events observed by the device other than intentional gesturing by the user\. 
		- indirect motion associated with operating a keyboard, pointer, or assistive technology\. 
3. For each functionality that can be triggered by motion actuation verify that both of the following are true: 
	- motion actuation can be disabled AND 
	- the functionality can be operated without using motion 
4. __Exceptions:__ 
	- __Essential__: The motion is essential for the function and doing so would invalidate the activity\. Example, a pedometer that relies on device motion to count steps\. 
	- __Supported Interface:__ The motion is used to operate functionality through an accessibility supported interface\. Example: a person with a motor disability using a form of assistive technology that is based on tracking head or eye movement as a form of input\. 
5. __Motion Actuation Double Check:__ Because motion actuation can be difficult to discover, double check by asking the developers of this page if they implemented any functionality that can be triggered by motion actuation\. Document this question and answer in writing for this assessment\.

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- Motion actuation cannot be disabled\. \(motion\-actuation\-cannot\-disable, "issue", serious\)
- Motion actuation disrupts or disables system level features\. \(motion\-actuation\-disrupts\-system, "issue", serious\)
- Functionality can only be activated via motion actuation \(such as shaking or tilting the device\)\. \(motion\-actuation\-required, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

__1\.2\.1\.a Text Transcript \(Prerecorded Audio Only\)__

__Overview__

- General \- Prerecorded audio\-only files must come with a descriptive text transcript\.
- HTML \- Prerecorded audio\-only files such as mp3 files and audio podcasts must come with a descriptive text transcript\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Hearing or hearing assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Locate any audio\-only media files\.
2. Ensure there is a transcript available for each audio\-only media file\.
3. Verify that the transcript: 
	1. Is available to all users
	2. Includes all essential dialogue
	3. Identifies the speakers
	4. Describes all essential sound effects
4. __Reality Check:__ I can just read the transcript and not miss any important audio content\.

__Representative Testing of Audio\-Only Content__

For sites with significant amounts of audio\-only content it is very time consuming to test for the accuracy of transcripts if you listened to every minute of every audio\-only file to make sure the transcripts are accurate\. For efficiency purposes, you can listen to just enough of the audio \(15\-30 seconds\) to confirm the existence of the appropriate transcript\.

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- A text transcript is provided, but it is incorrect or inadequate\. \(text\-transcript\-inadequate, "issue", serious\)
- A text transcript is provided but some of the dialogue is missing\. \(text\-transcript\-incomplete, "issue", serious\)
- No text transcript is provided\. \(text\-transcript\-missing, "issue", critical\)
- A text transcript is provided but it doesn't describe all important sound effects\. \(text\-transcript\-undescribed\-effects, "issue", serious\)
- A text transcript is provided but it doesn't identify all the speakers\. \(text\-transcript\-unidentified\-speakers, "issue", moderate\)

__Best Practices__

- Transcript is easy to find \- The text transcript provided is easy to find because it is next to the audio or video media\. \(text\-transcript\-hard\-to\-find, "best\-practice", moderate, October 23, 2019\)

__1\.2\.2\.a Captions \(Prerecorded\)__

__Overview__

- General \- Prerecorded multimedia files must come with text\-based synchronized captions\.
- HTML \- Prerecorded multimedia files must come with text\-based synchronized captions\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist
- Hearing or hearing assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Locate any multimedia \(video with audio\) files\. \(For audio\-only or video\-only media files, see Checkpoints 1\.2\.1\.a and 1\.2\.1\.b\)\.
2. Ensure captions are available\. Captions may be closed \(can be turned on and off in the video player\) or open \(always on\)\.
3. Verify that the captions: 
	- Are visible\.
	- Are synchronized with the video track\.
	- Identify speakers whose identity is not apparent when looking at the screen without sound but is understood by people with hearing\.
	- Provide full text transcripts of all dialogues\.
	- Describe important music and sound effects\.
4. __Reality Check:__ I can turn off the volume and read the captions and not miss any important audio content\.

__Exception__

__Captions are not required when the synchronized media is an alternate presentation of information that is already available in text on the page\.__ For example, if information on a page is accompanied by a video that presents no more information than is already presented in text, but is easier for people with dyslexia to understand, then it would not need to be captioned since the information is already presented on the page in text or in text alternatives \(e\.g\., for images\)\.

__Representative Testing of Captions for Video with Audio__

For sites with significant amounts of video content, it is very time\-consuming to test for the accuracy of captions if you watched every minute of every video to make sure the captions are accurate\. For efficiency purposes, you can watch just enough of the video \(30\-60 seconds\) to confirm the existence of the appropriate captions and whether or not they are synchronized\.

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- Captions are provided for recorded media, but they are incorrect or inadequate\. \(captions\-inadequate, "issue", serious\)
- Captions are provided for recorded media, but some of the dialogue is missing\. \(captions\-incomplete, "issue", serious\)
- Captions are not provided for the recorded multimedia content\. \(captions\-missing, "issue", critical\)
- Captions are provided for recorded multimedia, but they are difficult to read due to poor contrast between the captions and their background\. \(captions\-not\-clearly\-visible, "issue", serious\)
- Captions are provided for recorded multimedia, but they are not synchronized with the multimedia\. \(captions\-not\-synchronized, "issue", serious\)
- Captions are provided for recorded multimedia, but they do not describe all important sound effects\. \(captions\-undescribed\-effects, "issue", serious\)
- Captions are provided for recorded multimedia, but they do not identify all the speakers\. \(captions\-unidentified\-speakers, "issue", moderate\)

__Best Practices__

- Transcripts for video \- A full text transcript should be available that includes information that would be in both the caption and video description, so that it is a complete representation of the material\. \(video\-transcript\-missing, "best\-practice", serious, June 2, 2016\)

__1\.2\.4\.a Captions \(Live\)__

__Overview__

- General \- All live multimedia that contains audio must come with text\-based synchronized captions\.
- HTML \- All live multimedia that contains audio must come with text\-based synchronized captions\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist
- Hearing or hearing assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Locate any LIVE multimedia \(video with audio\) content\. \(For prerecorded multimedia files, see Checkpoint 1\.2\.2\.a\)\.
2. Ensure captions are available\. Captions may be closed \(can be turned on and off in the video player\) or open \(always on\)\.
3. Verify that the captions: 
	- Are visible\.
	- Are synchronized with the video track\.
	- Identify speakers whose identity is not apparent when looking at the screen without sound but is understood by people with hearing\.
	- Provide full text transcripts of all dialogues\.
	- Describe important music and sound effects\.
4. __Reality Check:__ I can turn off the volume and read the captions and not miss any important audio content\.

__Representative Testing of Captions for Live Video with Audio__

For sites with significant amounts of live video content, it is very time\-consuming to test for the accuracy of captions if you watched every minute of every video to make sure the captions are accurate\. For efficiency purposes, you can watch just enough of the video \(30\-60 seconds\) to confirm the existence of the appropriate captions and whether or not they are synchronized\.

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- Captions are provided for live media, but they are incorrect or inadequate\. \(captions\-inadequate\-live, "issue", serious\)
- Captions are provided for live media, but some of the dialogue is missing\. \(captions\-incomplete\-live, "issue", serious\)
- Captions are not provided for the live multimedia content\. \(captions\-missing\-live, "issue", critical\)
- Captions are provided for live multimedia, but they are difficult to read due to poor contrast between the captions and their background\. \(captions\-not\-clearly\-visible\-live, "issue", serious\)
- Captions are provided for live multimedia, but they are not synchronized with the multimedia\. \(captions\-not\-synchronized\-live, "issue", serious\)
- Captions are provided for live multimedia, but they do not describe all important sound effects\. \(captions\-undescribed\-effects\-live, "issue", serious\)
- Captions are provided for live multimedia, but they do not identify all the speakers\. \(captions\-unidentified\-speakers\-live, "issue", moderate\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

__1\.2\.5\.a Audio Descriptions \(Prerecorded\)__

__Overview__

- General \- Prerecorded multimedia files containing essential information that is only conveyed visually must come with a synchronized soundtrack with audio description in which gaps in dialogue are used to convey essential information regarding the visual content\.
- HTML \- Prerecorded multimedia files containing essential information that is only conveyed visually must come with a synchronized soundtrack with audio description in which gaps in dialogue are used to convey essential information regarding the visual content\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist
- Hearing or hearing assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Locate any multimedia \(video with audio\) files\. \(For audio\-only or video\-only media files, see Checkpoints 1\.2\.1\.a and 1\.2\.1\.b\)\.
2. If the video content does not provide additional information not perceived through the audio \(for example, a reporter simply reading a news story\), an audio description is NOT required\.
3. If the video content does provide additional information not perceived through the audio, an audio description is required\. Verify that a synchronized soundtrack with an audio description conveys the essential information of any important visual content\.
4. __Reality Check:__ I can turn on the Audio Descriptions track, close my eyes and listen to the video and not miss any important visual content\.

__Things to think about__

- If there is visual text embedded in the video \(such as the video title or speaker name\), and that text is also available in real text in the page, consider that as an accessible version of the content that satisfies this checkpoint\.
- Representative Testing: For sites with significant amounts of multi\-media content it is very time consuming to test for the accuracy of audio descriptions if you watched every minute of every video to make sure the audio description is accurate\. For representative testing, select a video portion that is typical of the video\(s\) you are reviewing and watch it to ensure the audio description is accurate\.
- How 1\.2\.3\.a Text or Audio Description and 1\.2\.5\.a Audio Descriptions relate: 
	- If there is no Audio Description AND no Text Description, both 1\.2\.3\.a and 1\.2\.5\.a are Failed\.
	- If there is ONLY a Text Description, 1\.2\.3\.a is Passed and 1\.2\.5\.a is Failed\.
	- If there is ONLY an Audio Description, both 1\.2\.3\.a and 1\.2\.5\.a are Passed\.
	- If there is BOTH an Audio Description AND a Text Description, both 1\.2\.3\.a and 1\.2\.5\.a are Passed\.

__Examples__

A great example of a video with audio description can be found at [3playmedia Audio Description Examples [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.3playmedia.com/solutions/how-it-works/how-to-guides/audio-description-how-to-guides/audio-description-examples/)](https://www.3playmedia.com/solutions/how-it-works/how-to-guides/audio-description-how-to-guides/audio-description-examples/)\. 

__Note:__ Press the AD button on to hear the audio description\. The narrator voice you hear is not part of the original video\. That narrator voice is the audio description\. Press the AD button again to turn off the audio description \(AD\) track\. 

__Issue Descriptions__

- The audio description provided is not correct or is inadequate\. \(audio\-description\-inadequate\-aa, "issue", serious\)
- An audio description is not provided\. \(audio\-description\-missing, "issue", critical\)

__Best Practices__

- Audio description is easy to find \- The audio description provided is easy to find because it is next to the video\. \(audio\-description\-hard\-to\-find, "best\-practice", moderate, October 23, 2019\)

__1\.2\.3\.a — Text or Audio Description \(Prerecorded Video – with Dialogue\)__

__Overview__

- General \- Prerecorded multimedia files containing essential information that is only conveyed visually must come with a full text alternative describing the important visual details OR an audio description track\.
- HTML \- Prerecorded multimedia files containing essential information that is only conveyed visually must come with a full text alternative describing the important visual details OR an audio description track\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist
- Hearing or hearing assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Locate any multimedia \(video with audio\) files\. \(For audio\-only or video\-only media files, see Checkpoints 1\.2\.1\.a and 1\.2\.1\.b\)\.
2. If the video content does not provide additional information not perceived through the audio \(for example, a reporter simply reading a news story\), an audio or text description is NOT required\.
3. If the video content does provide additional information not perceived through the audio, an audio or text description is required\. Verify that one of the following is present: 
	1. __Audio Description:__ A synchronized soundtrack with an audio description conveys the essential information of any important visual\-only content\. __OR__
	2. __Text Description:__ A full text description is provided to convey important scene changes, character expressions, and any essential visual\-only information\.

__Things to think about__

- If there is visual text embedded in the video \(such as the video title or speaker name\), and that text is also available in real text in the page, consider that as an accessible version of the content that satisfies this checkpoint\.
- Representative Testing: For sites with significant amounts of multi\-media content it is very time consuming to test for the accuracy of audio or text descriptions if you watched every minute of every video to make sure the existing audio or text descriptions \(if any\) are accurate\. For representative testing, select a video portion that is typical of the video\(s\) you are reviewing and watch it to ensure the existing audio or text description is accurate\.
- How 1\.2\.3\.a Text or Audio Description and 1\.2\.5\.a Audio Descriptions relate: 
	- If there is no Audio Description AND no Text Description, both 1\.2\.3\.a and 1\.2\.5\.a are Failed\.
	- If there is ONLY a Text Description, 1\.2\.3\.a is Passed and 1\.2\.5\.a is Failed\.
	- If there is ONLY an Audio Description, both 1\.2\.3\.a and 1\.2\.5\.a are Passed\.
	- If there is BOTH an Audio Description AND a Text Description, both 1\.2\.3\.a and 1\.2\.5\.a are Passed\.

__Examples__

Examples of audio description on videos with dialogue [http://www\.audiodescribe\.com/samples/ [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](http://www.audiodescribe.com/samples/)](http://www.audiodescribe.com/samples/)

__Note:__ The audio description \(AD\) in these samples is turned on by default and cannot be turned off\. Ideally, the AD track should be a separate sound track, so users can decide to turn it on or off as needed\. 

__Issue Descriptions__

- The audio description provided is not correct or is inadequate\. \(audio\-description\-inadequate, "issue", serious\)
- Neither a text description nor audio description is available for multimedia content\. \(nonvisual\-description\-missing\-multimedia, "issue", serious\)
- The text description provided is not correct or is inadequate\. \(text\-alternative\-inadequate, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

- Audio description is easy to find \- The audio description provided is easy to find because it is next to the video\. \(audio\-description\-hard\-to\-find, "best\-practice", moderate, October 23, 2019\)
- Transcript is easy to find \- The text transcript provided is easy to find because it is next to the audio or video media\. \(text\-transcript\-hard\-to\-find, "best\-practice", moderate, October 23, 2019\)

__1\.4\.2\.a Audio Control__

__Overview__

- General \- A mechanism must be provided to stop, pause, mute, or adjust volume for audio that automatically plays for more than 3 seconds\.
- HTML \- A mechanism must be provided to stop, pause, mute, or adjust volume for audio that automatically plays on a page for more than 3 seconds\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Hearing or hearing assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Locate the multimedia\.
2. If the multimedia automatically starts playing audio \(that lasts for more than 3 seconds\), verify that: 
	- The audio can __easily__ be stopped, paused, muted, __OR__ volume is adjustable by keyboard alone 
	- Any functionality provided to control the audio that plays automatically on the page does not control overall system volume\. The audio volume control should function independent to overall system volume\.
3. __Reality Check:__ Multimedia with audio does not automatically start \(if it lasts more than 3 seconds\), __OR__ if the multimedia with audio does start automatically, it is easy for me to stop, pause, mute or decrease volume using only the keyboard\.

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- Audio starts playing automatically, lasts more than 3 seconds, and does not have an accessible mechanism to stop, pause, mute or adjust the volume of the audio\. \(audio\-autoplay, "issue", critical\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

